{"name":"Sea Star","hex":"#4d939a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea star","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A starfish (class Asteroidea).","A star used for navigation or guidance at sea."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea_star"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sea Star (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Sea Star is a Canadian short drama film, directed by Tyler Mckenzie Evans and released in 2025. The film stars John Phillips as Chris, a middle-aged Black Canadian man who is taking swimming lessons for the first time, while confronting the generational trauma and unresolved grief that have complicated the Black Canadian and African-American communities' relationship with the activity."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_Star_(film)","wikidata":"Q138856459"},{"title":"Starfish","blocks":[{"text":"Starfish or sea stars are a class of echinoderms generally shaped like a star polygon. Starfish are also known as asteroids because they form the taxonomic class Asteroidea. About 1,900 species of starfish live on the seabed, and are found in all the world's oceans, from warm, tropical zones to frigid, polar regions. They can occur from the intertidal zone down to abyssal depths, at 6,000 m (20,000 ft) below the surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Starfish_montage.png/330px-Starfish_montage.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starfish","wikidata":"Q25349"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"},{"title":"Star","blocks":[{"text":"A star is a luminous spheroid of plasma held together by self-gravity. The nearest star to Earth is the Sun. Many other stars are visible to the naked eye at night; their immense distances from Earth make them appear as fixed points of light. The most prominent stars have been categorised into constellations and asterisms, and many of the brightest stars have proper names. Astronomers have assembled star catalogues that identify the known stars and provide standardized stellar designations. The observable universe contains an estimated 1022 to 1024 stars. Only about 4,000 of these stars are visible to the naked eye—all within the Milky Way galaxy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star","wikidata":"Q523"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg/330px-Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg/960px-Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg/330px-Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALuidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Astropecten_aranciacus_Naxos08_1775_dett.jp